```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__codex_apps__sites_create_site(args: {
  // Why this Site is being created: user_requested when the user asked for a Site; proactive when the assistant chose to create one without that request; unknown when the original intent is unavailable. Preserve the original user's intent when work is delegated; an agent's build instruction is not a user request.
  creation_intent?: "user_requested" | "proactive" | "unknown";
  // Optional user-facing description of the site.
  description?: string | null;
  // Set true only when this Site needs workspace connector/plugin access. Omit for ordinary Sites. Subject to workspace BYOP eligibility.
  enable_plugins?: boolean | null;
  // Request automatic private publication after Git push when enrolled in the experiment; otherwise create normally. Build and repair locally first. Only skip explicit save/deploy when the returned source_repository_credential.publish_on_push_accepted is true. If false, use the existing explicit publishing flow. Recover a missing credential for the same project before pushing. Always confirm deployment success before reporting it.
  publish_on_push?: "private" | null;
  // Unique URL slug for the site. Start with a lowercase ASCII letter and use only lowercase ASCII letters, digits, and single hyphens. Do not use leading, trailing, or consecutive hyphens, a reserved Sites slug, or a slug already used by another site.
  slug: string;
  // User-facing title for the site.
  title: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{
  auth_client_id: string | null;
  created_at: string;
  current_live_url: string | null;
  current_preview_url: string | null;
  description: string | null;
  disabled_by?: "workspace_admin" | "openai" | null;
  // Generated Site origin for the current project and workspace route. Use it for absolute Site URLs needed before publication; it does not mean the Site is live. The source repository's remote_url is a Git endpoint, not the Site origin.
  expected_url?: string | null;
  // Opaque site project ID. Pass this exact value as project_id.
  id: string;
  latest_edit_context?: { chatgpt_conversation_id?: string | null; codex_thread_id?: string | null; } | null;
  latest_version_number: number;
  screenshot_url: string | null;
  slug: string;
  // Short-lived source repository write credential when requested.
  source_repository_credential?: {
  // AppGen AppRepository id.
  app_repository_id: string;
  // Git authentication mode for the token.
  auth_mode: string;
  // Default branch the client should push.
  branch: string;
  // Source repository provider.
  provider: string;
  // Whether this response confirms an accepted automatic private publication window. If true, push before publish_on_push_expires_at and check the matching version's deployment_id and deployment status; do not separately save/deploy. If false, Site creation and write-credential callers must use the existing explicit publishing flow. False does not cancel an earlier window: reconcile any existing deployment before retrying publication.
  publish_on_push_accepted?: boolean;
  // Until this timestamp, the owner has authorized private publication of pushes to this branch. Null neither authorizes nor cancels a window. After expiry, opt in again through create_source_repository_write_credential.
  publish_on_push_expires_at?: string | null;
  // Git remote URL without embedded credentials.
  remote_url: string;
  // Provider repository name bound to the AppGen project.
  repository: string;
  // Short-lived repo-scoped Git token.
  token: string;
  // Token expiration timestamp when provided.
  token_expires_at: string;
} | null;
  status: "creating" | "active" | "suspended" | "deleting";
  title: string;
  updated_at: string;
}>>; };
```"},{"name":"mcp__prismic__query_documents","description":"Find every document matching structured filters — custom type, locales, statuses, releaseIds, tags, dates, and field values. Unlike `search_documents`, results are exhaustive, not a ranked sample. Reach for this whenever the user asks for documents by a field's value or which documents use an asset. A complete query is the answer — do not follow it with `search_documents` to list or count the type.

REQUIRE `where` FOR FIELD VALUES:
Most field types are queryable — Select, Text, Color, Boolean, UID, IntegrationFields, Image, Embed, Link, Number, Range, Date, Timestamp and GeoPoint — top-level, inside a group, or inside a slice (its primary or items). Rich Text, Table and Separator support only `has`/`missing`. An Image field path and IntegrationFields match an opaque id (the origin asset id / the integration item id), never a URL. Image alt, credits, and named thumbnails, and Link labels and variants, use the suffixes below. Number/Range collapse spellings via JS `Number` (`"10"` and `"10.0"` match); Date/Timestamp collapse via `Date` parse to ISO, and Date `eq` needs a full `YYYY-MM-DD` — a year or year-month is INVALID_SHAPE (there is no range operator yet); GeoPoint atoms are `lat,lng` (latitude first, JS `String` of each coord) so `"48.8, 2.3"` matches `"48.8,2.3"` — GeoJSON is longitude-first and will miss. Setting only `scope` and skipping `where` returns every document of that type, which is the wrong answer to a field-value question.

BUILDING A PATH (model ids only — never a document's instance keys):
1. Call get_custom_type to find the slice zone's API id (often `slices`, but can be anything) and the slice type id you need. Shared slices appear there as `{type:"SharedSlice"}` stubs.
2. Call get_shared_slice for that slice type to find the field's API id under a variation's `primary` or `items`.
3. Compose the path, dropping `primary`, `items`, and the variation id entirely:
   - top-level field → `fieldId`
   - field inside a group → `groupId[].fieldId`
   - field inside a slice (primary or items) → `zoneId[sliceType].fieldId`
   - field inside a group that is itself inside a slice → `zoneId[sliceType].groupId[].fieldId`
   - a nestable LegacySlice that is itself a field → `zoneId[sliceType]`
   - nested image/embed/link inside Rich Text → append `{image}`, `{embed}`, or `{link}` to the Rich Text path (`body{image}`, `slices[hero].description{image}`, `slices[hero].description{embed}`, `body{link}`, `slices[hero].description{link}`, `slices[rich_text]{link}`)
   - nested hyperlinks inside a Table → append `{link}` to the Table path (`pricing{link}`, `slices[pricing]{link}`). `{image}`/`{embed}` are invalid on Table. Standalone Image and Link fields do not take a nested selector (`hero_image{link}` and `cta_link{link}` are INVALID_MODEL_PATH).
   - image alt and credits → append `@alt` or `@credits` (`hero_image@alt`, `body{image}@credits`, `slices[hero].image{mobile}@alt`). `@` is always last.
   - a named thumbnail on a standalone Image field → `{name}` from that field's `config.thumbnails` (`hero_image{mobile}`, `hero_image{mobile}@alt`). A name that is not `[A-Za-z0-9_-]+` is quoted, which is how whitespace is written: `hero_image{"Mobile 2x"}`. There is no any-thumbnail path — OR the names from the model. Rich Text images have no thumbnails.
   - link label → `@label` (`cta_link@label`, `body{link}@label`, `pricing{link}@label`). `@variant` is only on a Link field whose model declares `variants` (`cta_link@variant`). Nested links have no variant. A variant value outside that list is INVALID_SHAPE.
   - an empty `@alt`, `@credits`, `@label`, or `@variant` is omitted, including on a thumbnail (`hero_image{mobile}@alt`, `hero_image{mobile}@credits`). `has` on `hero_image` and `missing` on `hero_image@alt` means the image is set and its alt is empty. A set alt, credits, label, or variant stays queryable when the asset or link target is empty. See WHERE for repeated paths.
   - there is no star path. To find an asset anywhere, OR every Image field with every Rich Text `{image}` path from get_custom_type and get_shared_slice. A slice that has both an Image field and a Rich Text field needs both: `slices[hero].image` and `slices[hero].description{image}` (real API ids). Querying only the Image field misses documents that embed the asset in that Rich Text. The same OR pattern applies to every Embed field plus every Rich Text `{embed}` path, and every Link field plus every Rich Text/Table `{link}` path. Thumbnails are not included in that asset OR unless you add each `{name}` from the model.

EXAMPLES:
- top-level Select `theme` → `theme`
- group Select `target` in group `nav_items` → `nav_items[].target`
- Select `alignment` on shared slice `hero` in zone `slices` → `slices[hero].alignment`
- the same field if the zone's API id were `body` instead → `body[hero].alignment`
- Select `item_style` in that slice's repeatable items → `slices[hero].item_style` (`items` never appears in the path)
- Select `category` in group `questions` on slice `faq` → `slices[faq].questions[].category`
- LegacySlice group field `target` on `legacy_nav` → `slices[legacy_nav].target`
- nestable LegacySlice Select `color_pick` → `slices[color_pick]`
- top-level Boolean `is_featured` → `is_featured`
- Link `cta_link` → `cta_link`, matched by target document id, asset id, or external url
- asset usage on slice `hero`: OR `slices[hero].image` with `slices[hero].description{image}`. Omitting the `{image}` path misses embeds in that Rich Text.

Common mistakes — nested fields ARE queryable, these are syntax errors, not proof otherwise:
- `alignment` for a field that actually lives on a slice → PATH_NOT_FOUND (missing `zoneId[sliceType]`)
- `slices[hero].primary.alignment` → INVALID_MODEL_PATH (`primary` is never written)
- `slices[hero].items.item_style` → INVALID_MODEL_PATH (`items` is never written)
- `slices[hero$3f2a].alignment` → INSTANCE_PATH_NOT_ALLOWED (that's a document instance key; use the slice type id)
- `body[hero].alignment` when the zone's real API id is `slices` → PATH_NOT_FOUND (wrong zone id)

ON ERROR:
- PATH_NOT_FOUND, INVALID_MODEL_PATH, INSTANCE_PATH_NOT_ALLOWED mean the path is malformed. Re-check the model and retry with a corrected path — never treat this as proof the field can't be queried, and never fall back to enumerating every document instead.
- OPERATOR_UNSUPPORTED means the field type does not accept that operator, and the message lists the ones it does. Rich Text, Table and Separator accept only `has`/`missing` on the field itself — `eq` on `body` is wrong. Nested image/embed/link inside Rich Text uses `{image}`/`{embed}`/`{link}` on that path (`body{image}`), which accept eq/in/has/missing like standalone Image, Embed, and Link.
- FIELD_TYPE_UNSUPPORTED means the path resolved to a container (Group, Slices, Choice) rather than a leaf field. Re-check the path — do not treat this as proof the field value cannot be queried.
- INVALID_SHAPE means the value itself is not a valid form for that field type — a Boolean that is not `"true"`/`"false"`, a Date that is not a full `YYYY-MM-DD` (year or year-month is not a range), a GeoPoint that is not `lat,lng` (latitude first), a link `@variant` that is not in the model's variant list, an empty string, or a value too long to match exactly. Fix the value, do not change strategy.

WHERE:
Leaves are `eq`, `in`, `has`, `missing`; combine them with `and`/`or` (2+ children each). Requires `scope.customType`. Values are matched exactly after type-specific canonicalize, with no case folding, so `#FFF` and `#ffffff` are different colors. Leading and trailing whitespace is trimmed on Text, Color, Select, Number, Range, Date, Timestamp and GeoPoint. Booleans are `"true"` or `"false"`. Numbers collapse `"10"`/`"10.0"`/`"1e1"` via JS `Number`. Date is a full `YYYY-MM-DD`; `"2026"` or `"2026-08"` is INVALID_SHAPE, not every document in that year/month. GeoPoint is `lat,lng` (latitude first, JS `String` form); a longitude-first GeoJSON pair will not match. An empty value is omitted. On one field, `missing` means that value is empty and `has` means it is set. On a repeated path (a group, slice items, or several values in one Rich Text or Table), `has` means at least one instance is set and `missing` means every instance is empty. One empty instance among filled ones does not match `missing`. Text, `@alt`, `@credits`, `@label`, `@variant`, Select, Color, Number, Range, Date, Timestamp, UID, Integration Fields, Embed, an Image id, and a Link target use that rule. `has` on `hero_image` and `missing` on `hero_image@alt` means the image is set and its alt is empty. On a repeated image, that matches only when every alt is empty. The same applies to a thumbnail (`hero_image{mobile}@alt`, `hero_image{mobile}@credits`). A Boolean set to `false` is `has`, not `missing`, and matches `eq` with `"false"`. Rich Text and Table count as filled only when they have visible content, not leftover empty blocks or blank cells.

SCOPE:
Narrow by `customType`, `locales`, `statuses`, `releaseIds`, `tags`, and date bounds — the tighter the scope, the cheaper the query. `customType` is required whenever `where` is set. `tags` is a catalogue filter, not a field predicate: a document matches when it carries any listed tag. Tags match exactly and are case sensitive, so `launch` does not match `Launch`. The filter combines with `where`.

PAGINATION:
One HTTP call per invocation — pass the previous response's `page.nextCursor` back as `page.cursor` until `nextCursor` comes back null. `page.limit` is 1–1000 (default 100). A page can hold fewer than `limit` results and still carry a `nextCursor`, including an empty `results` array when every row on the page was stale, so keep paging until `nextCursor` is null. A repository with no ECQ index built yet returns an empty page whose `nextCursor` is null and no error, so retry after indexing rather than concluding there are no matches.

